---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Closing from "../components/Closing.astro";
import CompareTable from "../components/CompareTable.astro";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import Feature from "../components/Feature.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Languages from "../components/Languages.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import QuickStart from "../components/QuickStart.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import CompetingOptions from "../components/demos/CompetingOptions.astro";
import DocsTiles from "../components/demos/DocsTiles.astro";
import DrillDown from "../components/demos/DrillDown.astro";
import EvidenceTabs from "../components/demos/EvidenceTabs.astro";
import Footprint from "../components/demos/Footprint.astro";
import Grammars from "../components/demos/Grammars.astro";
import HistoryTimeline from "../components/demos/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import Measures from "../components/demos/Measures.astro";
import OwnModel from "../components/demos/OwnModel.astro";
import Pipeline from "../components/demos/Pipeline.astro";
import ProposalSwitch from "../components/demos/ProposalSwitch.astro";
import ClaimMarks from "../components/demos/ClaimMarks.astro";
import TalkRelay from "../components/demos/TalkRelay.astro";
import WalkthroughPlayer from "../components/demos/WalkthroughPlayer.astro";
import { links } from "../lib/links";
---

<Base
	title="Archboard: understand the code your agents write"
	description="Plan each change with your coding agent on a map of the system before it writes a line. Follow any feature into the code, and see every place where the map and the code disagree."
>
	<Nav />
	<main id="main">
		<Hero />
		<Languages />

		<Section id="understand" title="Follow a feature into the code.">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Open the map your agent wrote. Go from the whole system into one service, follow a payment
					message by message, and check each claim against the code.
				</p>
			</Fragment>

			<Feature title="Zoom from the system into a service" flip>
				<p>
					Open a module to see its parts and the connections that cross its boundary. Open a part to go
					deeper. Breadcrumbs lead back through the same model, and nothing is copied on the way.
				</p>
				<p>Select the Inventory service card to fly into it.</p>
				<DrillDown slot="demo" />
			</Feature>

			<Feature title="Follow one request, step by step" wide>
				<p>
					Ask what happens as a shopper pays, and get every path: who calls whom, what comes back, which
					branch runs when the card is declined, and which call retries until it lands. Your agent traces
					each message to the code that sends it.
				</p>
				<p>
					The architecture, the sequence and the flowchart are three readings of one model. They share
					its module identities, so a proposal that changes an exchange shows which messages changed.
				</p>
				<Grammars slot="demo" />
			</Feature>

			<EvidenceTabs />

			<Feature title="Find the parts worth investigating" wide>
				<p>
					Measures counts what each module exposes, what it hides and what it only passes through, and
					reads Git history for the modules that change together. Sort by any column to see where to
					read first.
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Many entries over few hidden parts can point to a shallow module.</li>
					<li>Unjoined counts modules that change together with no relationship between them.</li>
					<li>
						There is no score. The questions come from John Ousterhout's <em>A Philosophy of Software
						Design</em>, and you and your agent read the code and judge.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<Measures slot="demo" />
			</Feature>
		</Section>

		<Section id="talk" title="Talk to your architecture.">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Press Talk and ask out loud. The Codex voice answers in real time, and it does more than
					narrate. It reads the code, proposes a change to the architecture and builds it, like any
					Codex agent.
				</p>
				<p>
					Behind the voice, a fast coordinator reads the architecture and decides what the voice says.
					It hands sustained work to a full Codex thread in your checkout. That thread's approvals come
					to the pane. When running a command needs only your yes or no, you can say it aloud.
				</p>
			</Fragment>
			<div class="talk-stage" data-reveal>
				<TalkRelay />
				<p class="caption">
					An example conversation about the example shop. In Archboard you speak, and the voice answers
					aloud. Here the words play without sound.
				</p>
			</div>
		</Section>

		<Section id="plan" title="See the change before anyone builds it.">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Plan before your agent writes a line. Ask for a change and you get a proposal, drawn against
					what exists. New modules arrive green, changed ones orange, and removed ones stay on the page
					as red ghosts.
				</p>
				<p>Current architecture stays as it is until the proposal ships.</p>
			</Fragment>

			<div class="plan-stage" data-reveal>
				<ProposalSwitch />
				<p class="caption">
					The example shop's Queued fulfilment proposal: checkout answers once the payment starts, and a
					worker reserves the stock and sends the receipt. The counts are the answer of
					<code>archboard model compare</code>.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="sub-head" data-reveal>
				<h3 class="display feature-title">Weigh competing options side by side</h3>
				<p class="body">
					Branch two drafts from one starting point and open them in two panes. Both compare against the
					same root, and neither touches current architecture.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="options-wrap">
				<CompetingOptions />
			</div>

			<Feature title="Hold the build to the plan">
				<p>
					When your agent builds the change, the footprint sets the branch against its proposal. It
					reads the commits through each module's code binding and sorts every change into three lists.
				</p>
				<p>
					Work outside the plan shows up before review, and so does planned work the branch has not
					done yet.
				</p>
				<Footprint slot="demo" />
			</Feature>

			<Feature title="Keep a record that remembers why" flip>
				<p>
					When a proposal ships, your agent adopts it and records the reason. The architecture it
					replaces stays available as an exact checkpoint, and a shelved proposal keeps its reason too.
				</p>
				<p>
					Retained history lives in a separate Git archive, published only when you choose. Module files,
					flows, walkthroughs and active proposals stay in the repository for code review.
				</p>
				<HistoryTimeline slot="demo" />
			</Feature>
		</Section>

		<Section id="how" title="Nobody places a box.">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Your agent describes the system: its services, stores and queues, what contains what, and how
					they talk, in what order. Archboard handles the drawing. No file stores a box position or a
					layout rule.
				</p>
				<p>
					The agent that changes the code updates the model in the same task, and the code check shows
					where the two disagree. A renderer fix redraws every reading, history included.
				</p>
			</Fragment>
			<Pipeline />
			<p class="caption engine" data-reveal>
				One Rust core places the modules, routes the lines and shapes every word. The CLI and the browser
				run the same core as WebAssembly, with the same bundled fonts, so a picture reads the same in the
				app, in an SVG and in a PNG.
			</p>

			<Feature title="Agents work in plain sight">
				<p>
					An agent claims the subjects it plans to change. The shell marks the claimed model and each
					claimed row with a pulsing dot, and the panel says Claimed on a selected subject. Each mark
					names what the agent is doing.
				</p>
				<p>
					A claim is status, so it asks nothing of you. Every write names the exact revision it read,
					so the server refuses a stale write.
				</p>
				<ClaimMarks slot="demo" />
			</Feature>

			<div class="sub-head" data-reveal>
				<h3 class="display feature-title">Archboard draws itself</h3>
				<p class="body">
					Archboard keeps its own architecture in its repository and changes it through proposals, with
					the same commands a consumer uses.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div data-reveal>
				<OwnModel />
			</div>
		</Section>

		<Section id="share" title="Share what your agent found.">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Put a drawing in a design document, an ADR or a pull request, and it never goes stale. Or let
					a walkthrough explain a change one step at a time.
				</p>
			</Fragment>

			<DocsTiles />

			<div class="sub-head" data-reveal>
				<h3 class="display feature-title">Let the architecture explain itself</h3>
				<p class="body">
					A walkthrough is an authored tour through readings. Press Narrate and the Codex voice explains
					each step while the camera follows the selected parts. One command films the tour for a design
					review or a pull request.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="walk" data-reveal>
				<WalkthroughPlayer />
				<div class="walk-foot">
					<p class="caption">
						The example shop's own walkthrough, “How an order is placed today”. In Archboard the Codex
						voice speaks each step. Here the
						words play without sound. <a href={links.walkthroughs}>Hear a narrated pull-request video</a>.
					</p>
					<pre class="walk-cmd mono" tabindex="0"><span class="ps">$</span> archboard model record --checkpoint &lt;id&gt; --walkthrough "Why checkout stops waiting" --out pr-128.mp4</pre>
				</div>
			</div>
		</Section>

		<Section id="compare" title="Where Archboard fits.">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Most tools explain code after your agent wrote it. Archboard starts before the first line: your
					agent plans the change on a map of what exists, you choose, and the map stays bound to the code
					as it ships.
				</p>
			</Fragment>
			<CompareTable />
		</Section>

		<Section id="quick-start" title="Get started.">
			<QuickStart />
		</Section>

		<Section id="faq" title="Questions.">
			<Faq />
		</Section>

		<Closing />
	</main>
	<Footer />
</Base>

<style>
	.plan-stage,
	.talk-stage {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 16px;
	}
	.plan-stage .caption,
	.talk-stage .caption,
	.walk-foot .caption {
		max-width: 52em;
	}
	.caption code {
		font-size: 0.85em;
		color: var(--ink);
	}
	/* A heading inside a section, for a demonstration that is not a feature row. */
	.sub-head {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 4fr) minmax(0, 7fr);
		align-items: end;
		gap: 16px 96px;
		margin: clamp(88px, 10vw, 140px) 0 32px;
	}
	/* The renderer's technical detail sits under the pipeline, below the outcome it explains. */
	.engine {
		max-width: 52em;
		margin: 20px 0 clamp(88px, 10vw, 152px);
	}
	.options-wrap {
		margin-bottom: clamp(88px, 10vw, 152px);
	}
	.walk {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 16px;
	}
	.walk-foot {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: baseline;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 12px 32px;
	}
	.walk-cmd {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		color: var(--ink-muted);
		overflow-x: auto;
		max-width: 100%;
	}
	.ps {
		color: var(--gold-mark);
	}
	@media (max-width: 900px) {
		.sub-head {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		}
	}
</style>
